  <ul class="pts one">
    <li>The register is added up from the group’s own records, so nothing is typed in twice.</li>
    <li data-new="1">Each member sees their own passbook, and every member reads the group’s records.</li>
    <li data-new="1">Loans to members carry a schedule: the day of each payment, what was paid back and what is still owed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="beta" data-new="1"><b>In the beta</b> a group’s loans are open to its members to read, and one admin signs for the group on each of its records.</p>
  <p class="rest" data-new="1">A group has an account of its own, apart from yours. Officers act for it from the profile picture menu, and records made that way are the group’s. Members, admins and the owner are its people, and each member confirms their own lines. Start one from your own account, choose the kind nearest yours, and invite members by phone number, by name only, or from people already on Sawaza. More in <a href="/help#groups">Help</a>.</p>
</div></section>

<section class="sec deep" id="round"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="duo">
    <div class="txt">
      <p class="q">How does a cash round work?</p>
      <h2 class="h2">One pot, paid in by everybody and out to one person a cycle.</h2>
      <p>Everyone pays the same amount each cycle, one member takes the pot each turn, and it repeats until everyone has had theirs. Sawaza sends reminders before each due date and shows who has paid, at a glance.</p>
      <p data-new="1">Each pay-in is a record the member signs, so the pot is counted from what was actually recorded. Sawaza records the money; it never collects it or pays it out.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="fig" role="img" aria-label="Screen of a cash round card: collecting for Grace, UGX 300,000 of 600,000 paid in, three members still to pay, two paid, and Show all 6.">
